# Neck walk's tune (core/groove). The guitar riff was recorded for the game (2026-10-05); bass and drums under it.
# Written in F#; each round plays it in that round's note.
#
# A round is a phrase, two licks of two bars: the computer walks the note over the first (the call, a crash on its
# first beat, then the riff), the player walks it back over the second (the riff, then the end landing on the root);
# the crowd answers half a beat before the next round. The bar before the first round is the phrase's last.
#
# <bars> <part> <beat> <length> <note>    the guitar or the bass; beats from 0
# <bars> drums <beat> <drum>              kick, snare, hat, open (an open hi-hat), crash

tempo 138
key F#
phrase call riff riff end

# The riff: root, the fifth below, root, fifth, then up through the 6th and the 7th
call,riff guitar 0   1   F#3
call,riff guitar 1   1   C#3
call,riff guitar 2   0.5 F#3
call,riff guitar 2.5 0.5 C#3
call,riff guitar 3   0.5 D#3
call,riff guitar 3.5 0.5 F3
end       guitar 0   1   F#3
end       guitar 1   1   C#3
end       guitar 2   2   F#3

call,riff bass 0   0.75 F#1
call,riff bass 1.5 0.5  F#1
call,riff bass 2   0.75 C#2
call,riff bass 3   0.5  D#2
call,riff bass 3.5 0.5  F2
end       bass 0   1    F#1
end       bass 1   1    C#2
end       bass 2   2    F#1

call      drums 0   crash
call,riff drums 0   kick
call,riff drums 1   snare
call,riff drums 2   kick
call,riff drums 2.5 kick
call,riff drums 3   snare
riff      drums 0   hat
call,riff drums 0.5 hat
call,riff drums 1   hat
call,riff drums 1.5 hat
call,riff drums 2   hat
call,riff drums 2.5 hat
call,riff drums 3   hat
call,riff drums 3.5 open

end drums 0   kick
end drums 0   hat
end drums 0.5 hat
end drums 1   snare
end drums 1   hat
end drums 1.5 hat
end drums 2   kick
end drums 2   crash
end drums 3   snare
end drums 3.5 snare
